## 3.3 Hardware configuration must affect actual simulation

This is the most important part.

Selecting another robot must change its actual simulated behavior.

Implement the following:

| Configuration | Actual simulation effect |
|---|---|
| Maximum speed | Limits achievable robot velocity |
| Acceleration | Controls how quickly the robot accelerates |
| Braking | Changes stopping behavior |
| Physical dimensions | Changes collision footprint |
| Turning radius | Restricts turning behavior |
| Payload | Validates load capacity and affects performance if modeled |
| Battery capacity | Changes available operating energy |
| LiDAR range | Affects obstacle detection distance |
| Camera configuration | Affects available perception observations if modeled |
| Sensor noise | Changes perception uncertainty |
| Wheel configuration | Influences movement constraints where supported |

For example:

- A heavy logistics robot should have a larger collision footprint and lower acceleration.
- A compact delivery robot should have a smaller footprint and be able to navigate narrower spaces.
- A robot with shorter LiDAR range should detect obstacles later.
- A robot carrying a heavy payload should respect its load limit.

Do not implement these differences only in the user interface.

They must be consumed by the actual simulation engine.

Do not claim detailed physical realism unless implemented. Use clearly defined parameterized approximations where necessary.

